      "depth_vertical_datum": "NAVD88",
      "producer_method": [
        "Rough and smooth habitat areas determined by surface:planar area (SA:PA) ratio and/or vector ruggedness analyses of the bathymetric DEM (see Substrate_Analysis metadata for more information) were stratified by depth zones to produce depth-stratified habitat grids. Depth zones used were 0 - -30m, -30 - -100m, -100 - -200m, and <= -200m. Cell values in the depth-stratified habitat grid signify both depth zone and substrate as follows:\n\t0 \tDepth = 0- -30, Substrate = Smooth\n\t-1 \tDepth = 0- -30, Substrate = Rough\n\t-30 \tDepth = -30- -100, Substrate = Smooth\n\t-31\tDepth = -30- -100, Substrate = Rough\n\t-100\tDepth = -100 - -200, Substrate = Smooth\n-101\tDepth = -100 - -200, Substrate = Rough\n-200 \tDepth <= -200, Substrate = Smooth\n-201\tDepth <= -200, Substrate = Rough\n\nThe masking process used in the initial creation of the substrate data layer, which was used to minimize misclassification due to noise and artifacts, produces a conservative estimate of total rough (potential rock) substrate, especially in deeper locations such as canyon walls and the steeply sloped continental margin. In these areas, increased noise and high slope can confound the area ratio-based substrate classification, so a vector ruggedness measure was employed in place of the standard SA:PA ratio analyses.\n\n"
      ],
      "original_metadata_reviewed": true
    },
